INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-startup-jobs-13514-roles-20261104', 'global', 'USA',
'AI Startup Jobs: 13,514 Roles Open at 1,100 Companies in November 2026',
'',
'A fresh count updated on 4 November 2026 shows 1,100 AI startups advertising 13,514 roles between them, with a median base pay of $190,000 a year across the roles that state pay.',
'',
$$<p>On 4 November 2026, a fresh count of live job postings showed 1,100 AI startups advertising 13,514 roles between them, a snapshot that gives jobseekers a clear picture of where AI startup jobs are actually open right now. The AI startup jobs count, updated on 4 November 2026 by The Anti-Job Board from the companies' own careers pages, covers AI labs, AI products and agents, and the chips, clouds and data those systems run on. San Francisco, New York and London hold the largest shares, but 784 of the startups list remote roles, and the median base pay across the 3,926 roles that state a salary is $190,000 a year.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Source of the count</th><td>The Anti-Job Board, read from company careers pages and checked daily</td></tr>
<tr><th>Count date</th><td>4 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Companies with live roles</th><td>1,100 AI startups</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total live roles</th><td>13,514</td></tr>
<tr><th>Median base pay (roles stating pay)</th><td>$190,000 a year; middle half $153,000 to $235,000</td></tr>
<tr><th>Roles stating pay</th><td>3,926 of the 13,514</td></tr>
<tr><th>Remote roles</th><td>4,587 roles at 784 startups</td></tr>
<tr><th>Largest hiring hubs</th><td>San Francisco (2,849 roles), New York (1,654), London (772)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Most roles at one company</th><td>OpenAI (322), Shield AI (301), Anthropic (261), Speechify (255), Databricks (251)</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>AI startup jobs: where the hiring is concentrated</h2>
<p>The geography of the count is uneven. San Francisco accounts for 2,849 roles across 375 companies, New York for 1,654 roles across 328 companies, and London for 772 roles across 189 companies, followed by Toronto with 160 roles and Berlin with 112. The remote figure changes the picture for readers outside those cities: 4,587 roles, more than a third of the total, are listed as remote by 784 startups. A candidate in Nepal or India can therefore apply for a real share of this market, provided the posting itself allows applications from their country, since a remote label does not always mean worldwide eligibility.</p>
<p>The count's method matters before treating the numbers as a hiring promise. The Anti-Job Board reads each company's own careers page and counts a firm as an AI startup only when at least 60 per cent of its live postings describe a business that builds AI, or what AI runs on: models, AI products and agents, chips, GPU clouds and training data. Recruiters posting other companies' roles are left out. The total moves daily as postings open and close, so 13,514 is a reading for 4 November 2026, not a fixed quota.</p>
<h2>Who is hiring the most, and what they build</h2>
<p>Five companies sit well ahead of the rest. OpenAI leads with 322 live roles, followed by Shield AI with 301, Anthropic with 261, Speechify with 255 and Databricks with 251. The board describes Anthropic as an AI research company focused on safety, Speechify as a text-to-speech app that reads PDFs and books aloud, and Databricks as a data and AI platform for large companies. Below the top five, hiring spreads across legal AI platforms, infrastructure firms, GPU cloud providers, video security companies, healthcare platforms and defence firms, all hiring under the same AI label but asking for different skills.</p>
<p>The role families the board lists give a second clue about demand: machine learning engineer, AI engineer, forward deployed engineer, deployment strategist, GTM engineer and AI product manager. Deployment roles sit between building a model and getting it working inside a customer's business, and they often value product sense and client work alongside code.</p>
<h2>What the pay figures do, and do not, show</h2>
<p>The pay headline needs care. The $190,000 median covers base pay only, and only for the 3,926 roles that state pay, out of 13,514 in total. The middle half of those stated salaries runs from $153,000 to $235,000 a year, and the board notes the figure mixes every role and level together, from junior posts to senior leadership. Individual company medians vary widely, from $344,000 at the AI safety lab in the top three to $170,000 at the text-to-speech company. A median is not an offer, and equity, bonuses and location adjustments, which the base-pay figure leaves out, can change total pay a great deal. Ask for the full package in writing before judging one role against another.</p>
<p>Set this count against the wider picture. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/us-job-cuts-october-2026-ai-153074-20261103">Growentix reported on 3 November 2026 that United States employers announced 153,074 job cuts in October 2026, with AI cited in a large share of them</a>, while <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-india-hiring-october-naukri-jobspeak-20261103">Indian AI hiring rose in October even as overall hiring stayed uneven</a>. Large employers trimming roles and smaller AI firms adding staff can happen at the same time in the same market.</p>
<h2>AI startup jobs: how to apply, and what to do next</h2>
<p>Start from the company's own careers page, not from a forwarded post. The board's list is built from those pages, and applying through them avoids the re-posted listings that sometimes carry old closing dates. Before applying, check three things on the posting itself: the location rule, including whether remote means worldwide or limited to named countries; the work authorisation line, since many United States roles require existing authorisation and do not offer sponsorship; and the stated salary band, where one is given, so the conversation about pay starts from a published figure rather than a guess.</p>
<p>Tailor the application to the kind of firm, not just the job title. A lab will weigh publications and model work, a legal AI platform will weigh domain knowledge and customer deployments, and a chip or GPU cloud firm will weigh systems experience. Keep a record of every application, including the date and the posting text, because startup postings close without notice.</p>
<p>Finally, treat any request for payment with suspicion. Genuine AI startups do not charge candidates to apply, to interview, or to receive an offer, and they do not ask for fees for equipment, training or visa processing before employment begins. If a message claiming to come from a named startup asks for money, stop and contact the company through the address on its own website. The hiring is real, with 13,514 roles counted on 4 November 2026, but the safest route into it is the employer's own page, checked role by role.</p>
<p><small>Sources: The Anti-Job Board, "AI Startups Hiring Now: 1,100 Companies (November 2026)", updated 4 November 2026; company careers pages as counted by the board.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.theantijobboard.com/startups/hiring-now/ai'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'punjab-court-clerk-1270-posts-20261104', 'global', 'India',
'Punjab Court Clerk Recruitment 2026: 1,270 Posts, Applications Close 4 November',
'',
'The SSSC clerk recruitment for 1,270 posts in the District Courts of Punjab closes at 4:00 PM on 4 November 2026; applicants need a degree, Punjabi at Matriculation level and computer proficiency.',
'',
$$<p>On 4 November 2026, the last day to apply arrives for one of the largest court recruitments of the year in India, as the Punjab Court Clerk Recruitment 2026 closes at 4:00 PM for 1,270 Clerk posts in the District Courts of Punjab. The Punjab Court Clerk Recruitment 2026, run by the Society for Centralised Recruitment of Staff in Subordinate Courts (SSSC) under the High Court of Punjab and Haryana at Chandigarh, opened on 7 October 2026 under Employment Notice No. 37C/SSSC/PB/2026. Only candidates who hold a degree, passed Matriculation with Punjabi as a subject, and can work with computers should apply, and the application, including payment, must be complete before the 4:00 PM cut-off, since no other mode of application is accepted.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Recruiting body</th><td>Society for Centralised Recruitment of Staff in Subordinate Courts (SSSC), under the High Court of Punjab and Haryana, Chandigarh</td></tr>
<tr><th>Post</th><td>Clerk, District Courts of Punjab</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total posts</th><td>1,270 (1,243 existing vacancies as on 31 August 2026 plus 27 anticipated vacancies up to 28 February 2027)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Advertisement</th><td>Employment Notice No. 37C/SSSC/PB/2026, released 3 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application window</th><td>7 October 2026, 4:00 PM, to 4 November 2026, 4:00 PM</td></tr>
<tr><th>Qualification</th><td>BA, BSc or equivalent degree; Matriculation with Punjabi as a subject; proficiency in computer operations</td></tr>
<tr><th>Qualification cut-off date</th><td>4 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Age limit</th><td>18 to 37 years as on 1 January 2026, with relaxations for reserved categories</td></tr>
<tr><th>Notified pay</th><td>Rs. 29,200 (Level 5) for fresh recruitment</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application fee</th><td>Rs. 1,200 for all other categories; Rs. 710 for SC, BC, OBC, ex-servicemen and EWS candidates of Punjab; Rs. 875 for PwBD candidates of Punjab</td></tr>
<tr><th>Selection</th><td>Computer Based Test (100 marks), then a qualifying Computer Proficiency Test</td></tr>
<tr><th>How to apply</th><td>Online only, through sssc.gov.in</td></tr>
<tr><th>Exam date</th><td>Not announced</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Punjab Court Clerk Recruitment 2026: who can apply</h2>
<p>The eligibility rules have a condition that will catch out some graduates. A degree alone is not enough. The notice requires a Bachelor of Arts, Bachelor of Science or equivalent degree from a recognised university, plus Matriculation passed with Punjabi as one of the subjects, plus proficiency in operating computers, and all of it must be held by 4 November 2026, the closing date. Candidates who studied Punjabi only informally, or who dropped it before Matriculation, do not meet the language rule, and the fee is non-refundable, so checking this point before paying matters more than usual.</p>
<p>The age limit is 18 to 37 years as on 1 January 2026. SC and BC or OBC candidates of Punjab receive five years of relaxation, PwBD candidates of Punjab ten years, and ex-servicemen of Punjab military service plus three years. Eligible in-service employees can apply up to 45 years. Candidates outside Punjab should note that relaxations apply only under Punjab domicile and certificate conditions, and reserved-category candidates from other states may be treated as Unreserved. Read that clause in the official notice before choosing a category.</p>
<p>The vacancy split shows where the posts sit: Unreserved or General 282, EWS of Punjab 143, SC (Balmikis and Mazhbi Sikhs) 170, SC (Others) 147, and BC or OBC of Punjab 139, with ex-servicemen categories together accounting for 248 posts and sportsman and PwBD categories making up the rest. Women-reserved vacancies are set within various categories. The notice says posts may rise or fall without prior notice, so 1,270 is the notified total, not a guarantee for any single category.</p>
<h2>Exam pattern, fee and the qualifying computer test</h2>
<p>Selection runs through a Computer Based Test of 100 marks over two hours, followed by a Computer Proficiency Test that is qualifying only. The written test carries 50 objective questions on General Knowledge for 50 marks, 20 objective questions on English Composition for 20 marks, and a subjective English Composition section for 30 marks covering a 150-word essay (10 marks), letter writing (5 marks), precis writing (5 marks) and translation from Punjabi to English (10 marks). One-fourth of a mark is deducted for each wrong objective answer, while unattempted questions carry no penalty, and normalisation may apply if the test runs in multiple shifts.</p>
<p>The qualifying marks are strict in a specific way: 33 per cent in each subject and 40 per cent overall, and the subjective English section is marked only for candidates who score at least 33 per cent in General Knowledge. General Knowledge is therefore the gate to the rest of the paper, and preparation that ignores it to focus on English writing risks leaving the subjective marks unmarked.</p>
<p>Candidates who clear the written test face the Computer Proficiency Test: a spreadsheet test of 10 marks in 10 minutes, needing at least 4 marks to pass, and an English typing test at a minimum speed of 30 words per minute, with mistakes counted as prescribed. Only candidates who pass the spreadsheet part have their typing checked. The proficiency marks do not add to final merit, which rests on the written test. Eligible candidates with benchmark disabilities who cannot type at the prescribed speed may seek exemption under the advertisement's conditions, with the required certificates.</p>
<h2>How to apply before 4:00 PM on 4 November 2026, and what to do next</h2>
<p>Apply only through the SSSC portal at sssc.gov.in, and finish both the form and the payment before 4:00 PM on 4 November 2026. The fee, including facilitation charges and the examination fee, is Rs. 1,200 for all other categories, Rs. 710 for SC, BC, OBC, ex-servicemen and EWS candidates of Punjab, and Rs. 875 for PwBD candidates of Punjab, paid online through the portal. Because the fee cannot be refunded, candidates should confirm their category, their Punjabi-at-Matriculation qualification and their age on 1 January 2026 before paying, and keep the payment receipt with the submitted form.</p>
<p>After submission, the work shifts to the test. The examination date, admit card release and venues have not been announced, and the notice says the test may be held anywhere in Northern India, with details published on the official website. Admit cards will be available online only, not by post, so candidates should watch sssc.gov.in and their registered email. Carry the admit card and identification documents to the centre.</p>
<p>The same care about the closing hour applies elsewhere. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/supreme-court-junior-court-attendant-130-posts-20261104">Growentix covered the Supreme Court Junior Court Attendant recruitment, open from 4 November 2026 to 25 November 2026</a>, and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/upessc-prt-assistant-teacher-12405-posts-20261103">the UPESSC assistant teacher recruitment for 12,405 posts also closed on 4 November 2026</a>. Court and school recruitments commonly close in the afternoon rather than at midnight, and a form left at the payment step after the cut-off does not count. For the Punjab clerk posts, that cut-off is 4:00 PM on 4 November 2026, and the official notice is the final word on eligibility.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Society for Centralised Recruitment of Staff in Subordinate Courts, Employment Notice No. 37C/SSSC/PB/2026, released 3 October 2026, as summarised by The Jobs Me and Today Government Jobs; official portal sssc.gov.in.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://thejobsme.com/jobs/punjab-court-clerk-recruitment-2026-1270-posts', 'https://www.sssc.gov.in/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'dubai-government-jobs-expats-45-roles-20261104', 'global', 'UAE',
'Dubai Government Jobs for Expats: Only 45 of 299 Roles Open to All Nationalities',
'',
'Of 299 Dubai government vacancies checked on 4 November 2026, only 45 are open to all nationalities, with the Roads and Transport Authority holding 29 of them.',
'',
$$<p>On 4 November 2026, a fresh check of Dubai's official government jobs portal gave expat jobseekers a blunt answer to a question they ask every day: of 299 Dubai government vacancies examined, only 45 are open to all nationalities, while 254 are reserved for UAE nationals only. The Dubai government jobs for expats picture, counted on 4 November 2026 from Dubai Careers (dubaicareers.ae), shows the Roads and Transport Authority as the largest hirer of expats, with 29 of those 45 open roles. Anyone applying from outside the UAE should therefore check the nationality line on each vacancy before spending time on an application, because most listings on the portal will not accept them.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Portal checked</th><td>Dubai Careers (dubaicareers.ae), the official Dubai Government jobs portal</td></tr>
<tr><th>Count date</th><td>4 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total vacancies shown</th><td>320 open vacancies; 299 checked under a hiring entity</td></tr>
<tr><th>Open to all nationalities</th><td>45 (about 15 per cent), with priority still given to UAE nationals</td></tr>
<tr><th>UAE nationals only</th><td>254 (about 85 per cent)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Biggest expat hirer</th><td>Roads and Transport Authority: 90 vacancies, 29 open to all nationalities</td></tr>
<tr><th>Next largest</th><td>Dubai Culture: 39 vacancies, 6 open to all nationalities</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application fee on the portal</th><td>None; applying on Dubai Careers is free</td></tr>
<tr><th>Visa</th><td>The hiring entity sponsors the employee's UAE residence visa, Emirates ID and medical test</td></tr>
<tr><th>Salary published on listings</th><td>Not published on most listings; senior specialist and chief roles have been reported at up to around AED 40,000 a month</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Dubai government jobs for expats: who is actually hiring</h2>
<p>The Roads and Transport Authority dominates the expat-accessible list, with 29 of its 90 vacancies open to all nationalities, including Senior Team Leader and Chief Specialist posts at the Talent Development Center, a Senior Engineer for Rail Network Permits and Services, and HR Business Affairs specialists. Dubai Culture follows with 6 open roles out of 39, including Corporate Excellence Specialist, Strategic Planning Senior Specialist, and section managers for education programmes, visual arts and commercial development.</p>
<p>Elsewhere the numbers thin out fast. Dubai Government itself lists 3 open roles out of 22, the Emirati Human Resources Development Council 2 out of 16, Dubai Air Navigation Services 2 out of 4 (both air traffic controller posts), and the Legal Affairs Department, the Department of Finance and the Government Human Resource Department 1 each. Dubai Media Incorporated, the Community Development Authority, the Civil Aviation Authority and the Women Establishment list none. The pattern reflects Emiratisation policy, under which UAE nationals receive priority.</p>
<p>The nationality rule is simple once stated plainly. A vacancy marked "UAE Only" cannot be filled by an expat. A vacancy marked "All Nationalities (Priority for UAE National)" can be filled by any expat with the right qualifications, but a qualified Emirati candidate will be preferred. There are no separate quotas by nationality or by gender: every role marked "All Nationalities" is open to women expats on the same terms. The check that matters is the nationality line on the individual vacancy, not the job title.</p>
<h2>What expats need to get hired, and what the pay picture looks like</h2>
<p>Most roles open to expats are specialist, senior or chief positions asking for several years of experience, in engineering, HR, finance, legal, culture and technology. Fresh graduates therefore face long odds on this portal: the count's guidance says freshers have better chances with government-owned companies, airlines and airports, and in temporary roles such as survey work. The Roads and Transport Authority follows the same pattern, while technician and workshop roles, when they appear, offer the better entry point.</p>
<p>Salary is the weakest part of the public information. Dubai Careers does not publish salaries on most listings, so applicants cannot compare roles on pay before applying. News reports on government roles open to expats in 2026 have quoted packages of up to around AED 40,000 a month for senior specialist and chief roles, often with housing and transport allowances, annual flights and health insurance, though these vary by entity and grade. Ask for the full breakdown in writing before accepting an offer, rather than treating a reported ceiling as a typical salary. For housing context, the guide cites median advertised rents in the third quarter of 2026 of about AED 72,000 a year in JVC, AED 105,000 in Business Bay and AED 180,000 in Downtown Dubai.</p>
<p>The visa side is clearer: every Dubai government entity that hires an expat sponsors the employee's UAE residence visa, Emirates ID and medical test, and UAE labour law puts recruitment and visa costs on the employer. A genuine offer should never ask the candidate to pay for their own work visa. Separately, a job seeker visa for searching from inside the UAE allows a stay of 60, 90 or 120 days without a sponsor, with an issue fee of AED 200, AED 300 or AED 400 respectively plus VAT, a refundable deposit of about AED 1,000 and service fees, bringing reported totals to around AED 1,500 to AED 1,800. Confirm fees with ICP, or GDRFA for Dubai, before paying.</p>
<h2>How to apply on Dubai Careers, and what to do next</h2>
<p>Apply only through dubaicareers.ae. Create a profile, upload a CV in English kept to two pages and matched to the job title wording where that is true, and filter by entity before opening each vacancy to check its nationality line. Answer the screening questions with care, since many roles ask for a minimum number of years of experience and a specific degree. Have a passport copy, degree certificates, experience letters and any professional licences ready: many employers ask for attested certificates at the offer stage, and some roles need an equivalency from the Ministry of Education, a step that can take weeks if left late.</p>
<p>Keep government jobs apart from government-owned companies, which hire expats in much larger numbers through their own careers pages: DEWA, Dubai Police for civilian and specialist roles, Dubai Health, Emirates Group and Dubai Airports. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/alas-emirates-ready-mix-abu-dhabi-20261026">Growentix has also covered private-sector Gulf hiring, including a ready-mix company in Abu Dhabi</a>. Mixing the two overstates how many true government roles an expat can reach.</p>
<p>Above all, use official sites only. Fake government job offers that ask for visa or processing fees are a common scam, and applying on Dubai Careers is free. If any person asks for money to secure a government job, that request is itself the warning sign. The route is narrow, with 45 of 299 vacancies open on 4 November 2026, but it is open to candidates who check the nationality line first and match the exact degree and experience asked for.</p>
<p><small>Sources: ViewIt, "Dubai Government Jobs for Expats 2026: Who Can Apply", vacancy counts checked from Dubai Careers (dubaicareers.ae) on 4 November 2026; salary and rent figures as reported in that guide.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://viewit.ae/blogs/dubai-government-jobs-for-expats-2026', 'https://dubaicareers.ae/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;
